 ## Known limitations & future work
 
-- **Neo4j Community is single-database / single-user.** A true read-only DB *role* isn't really available on Community, so the **tool layer (and the READ transaction) is the enforced boundary** — by design, and tested hard (see `tests/test_mcp_server.py`).
+- **Neo4j Community is single-database / single-user.** A true read-only DB *role* isn't really available on Community, so the **tool layer (and the READ transaction) is the enforced boundary**, by design, and tested hard (see `tests/test_mcp_server.py`).
 - **Engine portability.** Hunts are expressed in Cypher; porting to another graph engine means re-expressing the `QUERIES`. The tool *contract* (the five MCP tools) is engine-agnostic.
 - **Aggregate-hunt traceability.** Per-event hunts carry `event_id` + `channel` for direct `get_event` traceback; aggregate/count hunts (e.g. `event_coverage`, `lateral_tool_reuse`) return host + key sufficient for a follow-up `get_host_summary` / `query_graph`, but are not per-event by nature.
 - **EID 10 (Sysmon ProcessAccess) is contentless in this graph.** `SourceImage`/`TargetImage`/`GrantedAccess` were not parsed into node properties this load, so LSASS-targeting credential access can't be confirmed from the graph alone (it's established independently). See `docs/accuracy-report.md`.
-- **The `query_graph` lexical guard over-rejects — including some legitimate DFIR searches.** The write-word check scans the whole query text *including string literals*, so a pure read that searches command lines for anti-forensics indicators — e.g. `WHERE p.commandLine CONTAINS 'vssadmin delete shadows'`, `reg delete`, or `Set-MpPreference` — is refused (the literal contains `DELETE`/`SET`). This is **deliberate**: scanning literals is what also catches write verbs smuggled into apoc procedure string arguments, and for a read-only forensic server over-rejection is the safe failure direction — it can only block a read, never allow a write (the READ transaction, not the lexical list, is the real guarantee). The practical workaround is the constrained surface: named hunts via `run_hunt` (e.g. `log_cleared`, `powershell_script`) and `get_event` traceback, which don't pass through the lexical guard.
-- **4624 logon dedup is time-blind.** To collapse ~1.6M routine logons, ingest keeps only the *first* 4624 per `(target_user, source_ip, logon_type)` tuple — the dedup key has **no time component**. A repeat of an already-seen tuple at a later, anomalous time (e.g. a 02:00 logon from the same user/IP/type that previously appeared at 09:00) is dropped, so temporal hunts like `after_hours_logons` see first occurrences only. Low impact on the SRL-2018 intrusion (an external attacker's lateral movement creates *new* tuples, which are kept), but a real blind spot for insider / after-hours scenarios — disclosed here and in `docs/accuracy-report.md` §1 rather than discovered.
